        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Vector Equation of the Line Passing Through Two Given Points",
        "problem": "Find a vector equation of the line that passes through the points $C(-1,0,4)$ and $D(-7,2,11).$",
        "worked_solution": "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $C$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=c+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $c$ is the position vector of $C.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $C(-1,0,4)$ and $D(-7,2,11)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,11⟩-⟨-1,0,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
