        "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant After Switching Two Rows",
        "problem": "Suppose $C$ is a $9\\times 9$ matrix and $det(C)=-69.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{8}$ (switching the fifth and the eighth rows). What is the value of $det(D)?$",
        "worked_solution": "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-det(C) \\\\  & =-(-69) \\\\  & =69.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
