        "knowledge_point": "Identifying a Row Operation Given the Connection Between Two Determinants",
        "problem": "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=-60$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $1$st and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-60) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
